id: 28
event: tool_call
data: {"session_id":"1c047b6c-cb6b-4b06-9d0b-e14ca5330d2d","seq":28,"kind":"tool_call","payload":{"call":{"content":"# HTTP Status Lookup — Report\n\n## 1. Research: HTTP 418\n- **418 I'm a teapot** — the server refuses to brew coffee because it is, permanently, a teapot. An April Fools' joke from the Hyper Text Coffee Pot Control Protocol.\n- Defined in **RFC 2324** (Hyper Text Coffee Pot Control Protocol, April 1, 1994); later acknowledged in **RFC 9110** (HTTP Semantics, 2022), which reserves 418.\n- Sources:\n  - https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Status/418\n  - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2324\n  - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110\n\n## 2. What I built\n`/workspace/app`: `index.html` + `app.js` — an HTTP status lookup page covering 10+ codes (200, 201, 204, 301, 302, 400, 401, 403, 404, 418, 429, 500, 502, 503). Served with `python3 -m http.server 8000` in the background.\n\n## 3. Test result\nOpened http://localhost:8000, typed 418, clicked Look up. Page showed:\n> **418 I'm a teapot** — The server refuses to brew coffee because it is, permanently, a teapot (RFC 2324).\n\nScreenshot: `/workspace/screenshot.png`.\n\n## 4. Files\n- `/workspace/report.pdf` (this report, converted)\n- `/workspace/screenshot.png`\n","path":"/workspace/report.md","tool":"write_file"},"tool":"write_file"},"at":1793932251606}

